==== OEM easy installation ====

  - Remove all dots from the factory image filename (except the dot before file extension)
  - Upload and update the firmware via the original web interface
  - Two options are possible after the reboot:
    * OpenWrt - that's OK, the mission accomplished
    * Stock firmware (after 3 unsuccessful boots bootloader will switch the slot) - install Stock firmware (to switch booflag from Sercomm0 to Sercomm1) and then OpenWrt factory image.

==== OEM installation ====
  - Connect via SSH & Login to shell
  - See which slot the OS is running from
    - <tabbox FW OEM v1.0.xx>
<WRAP lo><code bash>
# hexdump -Cn 8 /dev/mtd3                                                       
00000000  53 65 72 63 6f 6d 6d 30                           |Sercomm0|          
00000008 
</code></WRAP>
<tabbox FW OEM v2.0.xx>
<WRAP lo><code bash>
# bootflag_utility -g    
[bootflag_utility.c:get_bootflag:52] bootflag=Sercomm0
get_bootflag=0
</code></WRAP>
</tabbox>
    - If **Sercomm0** - switch slot and reboot.
      - <tabbox FW OEM v1.0.xx>
<WRAP lo><code bash>
printf 1 | dd bs=1 seek=7 count=1 of=/dev/mtdblock3
reboot
</code></WRAP>
<tabbox FW OEM v2.0.xx>
<WRAP lo><code bash>
bootflag_utility -s 1
reboot
</code></WRAP>
</tabbox>
    - If **Sercomm1** - continue.
  - Remove dots from the OpenWrt factory image filename (except the dot before file extension)
  - Flash the *-factory.img image from the web interface.

